X('customs-clearance-process-india',[
 ('What happens to the cargo during clearance',['While the paperwork moves, the cargo itself sits in a customs-controlled area: a port terminal, a container freight station (CFS), an inland container depot (ICD) or an air cargo terminal. It cannot leave until the out of charge order is given, and the custodian will not release it until its own charges are paid.','For sea cargo the delivery order from the shipping line is the second key. Customs clears the goods, the line releases the container, and the terminal or depot lets it out. A delay at any of the three holds the cargo.']),
 ('Where time is usually lost',['A mismatch between invoice, packing list and bill of lading, even a small one in quantity or description.','A product that needs a permission nobody checked: BIS registration, an FSSAI licence, a wireless licence or a plant quarantine certificate.','Value queries on a low invoice price, or a classification the officer disagrees with.','Late filing. If the bill of entry is filed only after the ship arrives, free time at the port starts running with nothing happening.']),
 ('How to make it faster',['Send the draft documents to your broker before the supplier ships, not after. Most problems are cheap to fix at that stage.','Have the broker confirm the HS code, duty and permissions in writing before you pay the supplier.','Ask for advance filing, so the bill is assessed before the vessel or flight lands.','Know your free days with the shipping line and the depot, so you can see what each day of delay costs.'])],
 [('Who pays the duty, the importer or the broker?','The importer is liable for the duty. The broker pays it from funds the importer provides, usually through the ICEGATE payment system, and charges a fee for the service.')])
X('what-is-a-freight-forwarder',[
 ('What a forwarder does, step by step',['Takes your enquiry and quotes the whole route, not just the ocean or air leg.','Books space with the shipping line or airline, often through a consolidated contract that is cheaper than what a small shipper can get alone.','Prepares or checks the documents: bill of lading or airway bill, invoice, packing list, certificates.','Arranges pickup, export clearance, the main carriage, import clearance and delivery, or whichever of these you need.','Tracks the cargo and tells you when something changes.']),
 ('Forwarder, shipping line, NVOCC and customs broker',[('table',['Party','What it is'],[['Shipping line','Owns or operates the vessels and issues the master bill of lading'],['NVOCC','A carrier without its own ships. Issues its own house bill of lading'],['Freight forwarder','Arranges the whole movement for the shipper and may also act as NVOCC'],['Customs broker','Licensed to file bills of entry and shipping bills with Customs']],'One company can be several of these. YAM Logistics, for example, is both a forwarder and a licensed customs broker.')]),
 ('Why having both roles in one company matters',['If the forwarder is not a licensed broker, your customs work passes to a second company. That is another party to brief, another invoice and another place a document can wait. When one company does both, the person who booked the cargo also knows what customs needs, and can fix a problem before the cargo moves.'])],
 [('Do I need a freight forwarder to ship?','No law forces you to use one, but the shipping lines and airlines sell mostly to forwarders and large shippers. For most small and medium shippers a forwarder is cheaper and far simpler.')])
X('how-to-choose-a-freight-forwarder',[
 ('Questions to ask before you pick',['Which lanes do you ship every week? A forwarder strong on the Gulf may be average on Brazil.','Do you hold your own customs broker licence, or do you pass that to someone else?','Who will I speak to when something goes wrong, and are they reachable out of hours?','How do you quote: all-in, or base rate plus surcharges added later?','Can you show me a recent shipment of my type, with the documents?']),
 ('Reading a quote',['A low headline rate often hides origin and destination charges, documentation fees, fuel and peak-season surcharges. Ask for the quote on the same basis from each forwarder, for example door to port or port to port, with every line shown. Our guide to <a href="guides/ocean-freight-surcharges-explained" style="color:#0A6CC2">ocean freight surcharges</a> lists the usual ones.']),
 ('Red flags',['A price far below the others with no explanation.','No licence number, or a licence that cannot be checked.','Pressure to pay a large advance into a personal account.','Vague answers about who is the actual carrier.'])],
 [('Should I always pick the cheapest forwarder?','No. A cheap quote that leaves out charges, or a forwarder who disappears when cargo is held, costs more than a fair price from one who answers the phone.')])
X('customs-broker-role-and-licence',[
 ('What the licence is',['A customs broker in India works under a licence issued by Customs under the Customs Brokers Licensing Regulations. The licence is for a specific customs location, and a broker who works at more than one location needs to be registered there as well.','The people who actually sign and present documents at the customs house hold identity cards issued under the licence. These authorised persons are tested on customs law and procedure.']),
 ('What a broker may and may not do',['May file bills of entry and shipping bills, deal with officers, handle the cargo clearance and advise on classification and duty.','Must keep records for the period the law requires, and act only on the authority of the importer or exporter.','Must not let anyone else use the licence, and must exercise due diligence on the client and the documents.']),
 ('How to check a broker',['Ask for the licence number and the customs location it covers, and confirm it with the commissionerate. A broker with a real licence will give both without hesitation. See also how we describe <a href="customs-broker-in-delhi" style="color:#0A6CC2">our own licence</a>.'])],
 [('Can a broker be penalised for a mistake?','Yes. The regulations allow penalties, suspension or revocation of a licence for misconduct, so a broker has a real reason to get the filing right.')])
X('how-to-import-into-india-step-by-step',[
 ('Costs to budget for',['Cost of goods and international freight and insurance.','Customs duty and the other duties on the bill of entry, worked out on the assessable value. Use the <a href="customs-duty-calculator" style="color:#0A6CC2">duty calculator</a> for a first figure.','Port, CFS or depot charges, shipping line charges and inland transport.','Broker fees and any testing or certification cost.','Finance cost while the cargo is in transit and at the port.']),
 ('Example: a first import of machine parts',['A buyer in Delhi orders machine parts. Before paying the deposit they send the invoice to a broker, who gives the HS code and the duty. The parts need no licence, so the order goes ahead. The shipment lands at Nhava Sheva, the bill of entry is filed in advance, duty is paid, and the container moves by rail to an ICD near Delhi. The same steps, in the same order, apply to almost every import.'])],
 [('What is the first thing a new importer should do?','Get the IEC, and before ordering, ask a broker to confirm the HS code, the duty and whether any permission is needed.')])
X('bill-of-entry-explained',[
 ('Reading the duty on a bill of entry',['The bill lists each item with its assessable value, the basic customs duty, the social welfare surcharge, the IGST and any other duty. The total is the amount payable. Check each line against your own working before the bill is finalised, because errors found later need an amendment and can cost money.','The guides on <a href="guides/how-customs-duty-is-calculated-in-india" style="color:#0A6CC2">how duty is calculated</a> and <a href="guides/assessable-value-cif-landing-charges" style="color:#0A6CC2">assessable value</a> explain each line.']),
 ('Status and out of charge',['After filing, the bill moves through stages: assessment, payment, examination if selected, and the out of charge order. Your broker tracks the status on ICEGATE, and you can ask for it at any stage.','Once out of charge is given, the cargo can be released from the port or depot against the delivery order.'])],
 [('Where can I see my bill of entry status?','Your broker can show it from the customs system. Importers can also check the status of a bill of entry on the customs portal using the details of the bill.')])
X('how-customs-duty-is-calculated-in-india',[
 ('A worked example',[('table',['Step','Amount (INR)'],[['Assessable value (CIF)','1,000,000'],['Basic customs duty at an assumed 10%','100,000'],['Social welfare surcharge at 10% of the duty','10,000'],['Value for IGST (1,000,000 + 100,000 + 10,000)','1,110,000'],['IGST at an assumed 18%','199,800'],['Total duty payable','309,800']],'Illustrative only. Real rates depend on the HS code, notifications in force and any trade agreement. Check the rate for your product.')]),
 ('Why the order matters',['IGST is charged on the value plus the duties before it, which is why it comes last. Missing the surcharge or applying IGST to the wrong base is a common error in a manual working. The <a href="customs-duty-calculator" style="color:#0A6CC2">free calculator</a> does the sequence for you, and the <a href="import-duty/" style="color:#0A6CC2">import duty pages by HS code</a> show the rates for over a thousand products.']),
 ('Other duties that may apply',['Anti-dumping and safeguard duties on specific goods from specific countries, and cesses such as the agriculture infrastructure cess on some products. See the guides on <a href="guides/anti-dumping-duty-explained" style="color:#0A6CC2">anti-dumping duty</a> and <a href="guides/social-welfare-surcharge-explained" style="color:#0A6CC2">the surcharge</a>.'])],
 [('Can the duty rate change after I order?','Yes. Rates and exemptions are changed by the Budget and by notifications. The rate on the date the bill of entry is presented usually applies, so check close to shipping.')])
X('air-freight-chargeable-weight-and-rates',[
 ('A worked example',['A box of 60 x 50 x 40 cm weighs 20 kg. Volume is 60 x 50 x 40 = 120,000 cubic cm. Divided by 6,000 that is 20 kg volumetric weight, equal to the actual weight, so the chargeable weight is 20 kg. A lighter but larger box of 80 x 60 x 50 cm at 15 kg has 40 kg volumetric weight and is charged at 40 kg. Use the <a href="guides/volumetric-weight-air-freight" style="color:#0A6CC2">volumetric weight guide</a> for the full method.']),
 ('What sits on top of the base rate',['Fuel and security surcharges that change by airline and season.','Handling, documentation and airport terminal charges at origin and destination.','Customs clearance fees and delivery.','Special charges for dangerous goods, temperature control or oversized cargo.']),
 ('How to lower the cost',['Pack tighter, because chargeable weight is the weight that counts. Ship on the weekday when rates are lower where the lane allows. Consolidate small shipments, and compare against express if the cargo is small and urgent, see <a href="guides/express-courier-vs-air-freight" style="color:#0A6CC2">express vs air freight</a>.'])],
 [('Do all airlines use 6,000 as the divisor?','Most use 6,000 cubic cm per kg for general cargo, but some express carriers use 5,000. Ask the carrier before you quote your own customer.')])
X('air-freight-booking-steps',[
 ('What to send when you ask for a quote',['Origin and destination airport, cargo description and commodity, number of pieces with dimensions and weights, whether it is dangerous or temperature sensitive, the ready date and the Incoterm. Missing any of these forces a second round of questions.']),
 ('Cut-off times and tracking',['Airlines accept cargo until a cut-off time before the flight, and late cargo is rolled to the next one. Plan the pickup and the export clearance to arrive well before it. After booking, the <a href="tracking/" style="color:#0A6CC2">free airway bill tracking pages</a> show the flight status, and the <a href="air-cargo-status-codes" style="color:#0A6CC2">status codes</a> guide explains each code.']),
 ('Typical problems',['Documents arrive after the cut-off, the packing list does not match the cargo, or a dangerous goods item is not declared. Each of these can cost a flight.'])],
 [('Can I change the flight after booking?','Usually yes, subject to space and airline rules, but the later you change, the less likely it is that space remains.')])
X('air-freight-vs-sea-freight',[
 ('Comparing the two',[('table',['','Air','Sea'],[['Transit','Days','Weeks'],['Cost per kg','Several times higher','Much lower'],['Best for','Urgent, high value, perishable, light','Heavy, bulky, low value per kg'],['Risk','Lower handling time','More time exposed to delay and damage']],'')]),
 ('How to decide',['Work out the landed cost of each, including the cost of money while stock is in transit and the cost of running out. A part that stops a production line is cheap to fly. A pallet of tiles is cheap to ship.','Many shippers use both, sea for steady stock and air for emergencies. See <a href="air-freight-services-india" style="color:#0A6CC2">air</a> and <a href="sea-freight-services-india" style="color:#0A6CC2">sea freight services</a> for what we offer.'])],
 [('Is there anything between air and sea?','Yes. Sea-air combines an ocean leg to a hub with a flight onward, and rail is an option on some routes. Ask your forwarder what fits the lane.')])
